🐳 Breaking Down the Dockerfile
A Dockerfile is a plain text script containing a series of instructions that Docker uses to build a container image. Each instruction creates a new image layer, and these layers form the final container image.

🔍 Dockerfile Instructions (Explained)
🧱 FROM
Dockerfile
FROM ubuntu
Sets the base image for the container.

Must be the first instruction in any Dockerfile.

Here, it uses the official Ubuntu image from Docker Hub.

⚙️ RUN
Dockerfile
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y python3-pip
Executes shell commands inside the image.

Used for installing software, setting permissions, etc.

Chaining commands with && keeps the image lean by reducing unnecessary layers.

📁 COPY
Dockerfile
COPY ./src /opt/source-code
Copies files/directories from your local machine into the image.

Here, the source code is copied into /opt/source-code inside the container.

🚀 ENTRYPOINT
Dockerfile
ENTRYPOINT ["flask", "run", "--host=0.0.0.0"]
Specifies the default command to run when the container starts.

Often used for launching applications or scripts.

In some setups, you may also see environment variables set like:

Dockerfile

ENV FLASK_APP=/opt/source-code/app.py
📚 Docker’s Layered Architecture
Each Dockerfile instruction creates a new layer in the image:

Base layer (Ubuntu OS)

APT package updates & installations

Python package installations (via pip)

Copying source code

Setting the entrypoint

This architecture:

Enables caching and reusability

Makes builds faster after the first time

Helps minimize storage by only saving changes per layer

You can inspect layers using:
docker history <image_name>
# Example:
docker history accountname/my-custom-app
🏗️ Building Your Docker Image
To build your image from a Dockerfile:

docker build -t accountname/my-custom-app .
-t adds a tag (name:version).

. refers to the current directory, where your Dockerfile lives.

✅ Best Practices
Minimize RUN layers using command chaining: RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y ...

Use .dockerignore to avoid copying unnecessary files into the image.

Combine COPY and RUN efficiently to reduce final image size.


